NAG Fortran
===========

Introduction
------------

NAG Fortran is a compiler that is very good at checking your code (both during 
compile and at runtime). It is the only compiler that has the option
(-C=undefined) to check whether all variables used are properly initialized.
This is a very powerful option. It comes with a drawback though: all routines
used by the program need to be compiled with this option. This means that
standard libraries available in binary form only (for example Intel MKL),
are incompatible with this option.

Installation notes
------------------

After installing the compiler run the command

  nagfor -V

The version info should be something like:

NAG Fortran Compiler Release 7.2(Shin-Urayasu) Build 7202
Product NPMA872NA for Apple Arm Mac OSX 64-bit
Copyright 1990-2024 The Numerical Algorithms Group Ltd., Oxford, U.K.

It is advised to use the latest version 7.2, obtainable from
https://monet.nag.co.uk/compiler/r72download/.

If it does not run, include the bin directory of nagfor in the PATH
environment variable in your .bash_profile file.

The compiler needs a valid license file to run. Either create a license
file nag.key in your home directory or set the environment variable
NAG_KUSARI_FILE to point to the license file. For example, put the line

export NAG_KUSARI_FILE=/usr/local/lib/NAG_Fortran/license.txt

in your .bash_profile file. If you have a floating license, the file should
contain the license server:

SERVER servername:

where servername should be replaced by your license server name. Don't forget
to put the colon at the end. Alternatively, you can set the environment
variable NAG_KUSARI_FILE to the license server:

export NAG_KUSARI_FILE=servername:

If you use nagfor in a Linux client in VirtualBox or Docker container and you
need to contact the license server through a vpn, setup the vpn from within the
Linux client and not from the host machine.

Enter the command

  nagfor -xlicinfo

to check whether the license has been properly setup.

Copy the most recent Mdefs_distr.mk to Mdefs.mk in the root of tfem.
Edit the Mdefs.mk file:

1) Set FC = nagfor
2) To set checking on:
   a. uncomment the FCHECK macro for the nagfor compiler.
   b. Set FOPT = -O0, to compile faster.
   c. Remove "-C=undefined" from the FCHECK macro if you can't use this option
      (see Introduction). Otherwise, perform also the following steps.
   d. Download (*) Lapack from http://www.netlib.org/lapack/, copy the file
      make.inc.example to make.inc and modify make.inc as follows:

        FC = nagfor
        FFLAGS = -O -C=undefined -dcfuns
        FFLAGS_DRV = $(FFLAGS)
        FFLAGS_NOOPT = -O0 -C=undefined -dcfuns

        TIMER = INT_CPU_TIME

      On macOS add -fpp to FFLAGS and FFLAGS_NOOPT.
   e. Invoke the command:

      make blaslib lapacklib

   f. Put the files liblapack.a and librefblas.a in the subdirectory
      lib of the root of tfem.
   g. Set the macro LIBBLAS in Mdefs.mk as follows:

      LIBBLAS = -llapack -lrefblas

   h. Add a line to external/hsl2002/ma57ad.f according to the following diff
      file:
2164a2165
>       PHASE = 0

NOTE: runtime checking can be slow. Therefore, it is recommended to reduce the
size of your problem first.

NOTE (*): Do not use LAPACK version 3.12.0. It uses an intrinsic function that
is not part of the Fortran standard and is not supported by nagfor.
